{
  "version": 1,
  "note": "Original, interpretive architectural types for Constantinople circa 1200. Ordinary building plans, decorative details, craft equipment and frequencies are design analogies; none identifies an excavated parcel or counts the historical population. Surviving Byzantine churches support ecclesiastical vocabulary, but these unnamed neighborhood chapels are invented assemblies.",
  "dimensions": {
    "plot_width_m": 10.0,
    "plot_depth_m": 12.0,
    "floor_height_m": 3.2,
    "foundation_depth_m": 6.0,
    "near_detail_m": 650.0
  },
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": "domestic_analogy",
      "claim": "Asymmetrical courts, small adjoining rooms, mixed use and uneven-height wings are cautiously adapted from medieval Byzantine domestic archaeology. Constantinople ordinary housing is poorly preserved. Hipped and gabled roofs, timber shutters and porches are interpretive choices, not a measured capital-wide building code. Sanders, printed p. 3, supports an eleventh-century Corinth courtyard, well, stair and upper rooms. His adjoining shop complex was redated to the fourteenth century and is not copied as a circa-1200 storefront.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.babesch.org/new_site/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/BABESCH_Byvanck_Lecture_2016_Sanders.pdf"
      ],
      "confidence": "comparative"
    },
    {
      "id": "craft_analogy",
      "claim": "Mixed household and production courts are comparative Byzantine urban types. Pots, storage jars, hand looms, workbenches, covered hearths and bread ovens distinguish activities; their placements, counts and exact equipment forms are illustrative. No industrial chimneys or Ottoman shopfront facades are claimed. Sanders, printed p. 3, supports an eleventh-century Corinth courtyard, well, stair and upper rooms. His adjoining shop complex was redated to the fourteenth century and is not copied as a circa-1200 storefront.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.babesch.org/new_site/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/BABESCH_Byvanck_Lecture_2016_Sanders.pdf"
      ],
      "confidence": "comparative"
    },
    {
      "id": "church_analogy",
      "claim": "Basilican and cross-in-square massing, tiled roofs, raised drums, apses and restrained brick articulation draw on Byzantine architectural vocabulary. Generic chapel layouts, sites and frequencies are invented; they do not reconstruct an identified lost church.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://mcid.mcah.columbia.edu/istanbul-documentation-project/zeyrek-kilise-camii-pantokrator-monastery",
        "https://mcid.mcah.columbia.edu/istanbul-documentation-project/bodrum-camii-myrelaion-monastery"
      ],
      "confidence": "typological"
    },
    {
      "id": "water_analogy",
      "claim": "Simple wellheads, troughs and covered gathering areas are plausible water-access types. This is interpretive street furniture, not a claim that a named fountain or supply connection existed on the selected plot. Ward, Crow and Crapper discuss the city-scale water infrastructure, not a standard fountain form. Sanders supplies a comparative courtyard well.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.pure.ed.ac.uk/ws/files/46577281/Ward_Crow_Crapper_2017_JRA_WaterSupplyConstantinople_AM.pdf",
        "https://www.babesch.org/new_site/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/BABESCH_Byvanck_Lecture_2016_Sanders.pdf"
      ],
      "confidence": "interpretive"
    }
  ],
